# TAPEOUT — the Verilog dojo

A first-person 3D RPG that teaches Verilog. `src/tapeout.jsx` is a SINGLE FILE
(~670KB, ~14k lines) that must stay runnable both locally (Vite) and pasted
into a Claude.ai artifact.

## Hard constraints — do not violate

1. **Single file.** All game code lives in `src/tapeout.jsx` with exactly ONE
   `export default`. Do not split it into modules. `src/main.jsx` is the local
   entry point only and is NOT part of the game.
2. **three r128, core only.** No `three/examples/*`, no jsm imports, no
   OrbitControls, no EffectComposer. `THREE.CapsuleGeometry` does not exist in
   r128 — use Cylinder + Sphere. Post-processing is hand-rolled in
   `makePostFX` with ShaderMaterial + WebGLRenderTarget.
3. **No localStorage inside `tapeout.jsx`.** The artifact runtime forbids it.
   The game calls the async `window.storage` API and degrades to in-memory
   saves if absent. Local persistence comes from the shim in `src/main.jsx`.
4. **All audio is synthesized** via Web Audio. No asset files, ever.
5. **lucide-react** icons must be imported by name; `Map` must be aliased
   (`Map as MapIcon`) because it collides with the global.
6. **No new runtime dependencies.** react, react-dom, three, lucide-react only.

## Workflow

- Run `npm run gate` before considering any change done. It must print
  `GATE GREEN`. It runs: esbuild bundle -> layout validation (all 7 worlds) ->
  content validation (309 checks) -> visual harness (8 scenes) -> React smoke
  test -> artifact-compatibility check.
- The gate scripts import internals by appending an export line to a COPY of
  the source in `.gate/` — never add exports to `tapeout.jsx` itself.
- `npm run dev` for the actual game.

## Navigation

The file opens with a TABLE OF CONTENTS listing ~36 fenced sections. Grep a
section title to jump. Major regions: VERILOG ENGINE, CONTENT, DEBUG BAY CORE,
FLIGHT RECORDER, COMBAT SYSTEM, FAB CAMPUS, BIT MINES, TRAIL DUNGEON MODELS,
ULTRA POST PIPELINE, ULTRA FAB LAYER, APP SHELL.

## Gotchas learned the hard way

- **CodeScreen and the Forge share near-identical run() internals.** A
  find-and-replace targeting one will silently hit both. Scope edits to the
  function body, and always assert the match count is what you expect.
- **World layouts are rasterized at 2-unit cell centers.** Rect edges landing
  on odd coordinates create phantom walls that can seal a world. Keep world
  dimensions and offsets EVEN. `npm run gate:layout` catches this.
- **Gate colliders must span the full corridor opening**, or players walk
  around them.
- Station numbering (`ord`) comes from `stationSequence()` and must match
  between the 3D world and the 2D world screen — both derive from
  `challengesOf(w)`.
